1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,815
This is a dollar bill.

2
00:00:01,865 --> 00:00:04,545
It costs about four cents to make.

3
00:00:04,595 --> 00:00:08,317
And almost everything you were taught about it…

4
00:00:08,367 --> 00:00:09,040
is wrong.

5
00:00:09,439 --> 00:00:10,369
This is Jake.

6
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:11,649
And this is Ryan.

7
00:00:11,699 --> 00:00:14,060
They grew up on the same street.

8
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:14,964
Same school.

9
00:00:15,014 --> 00:00:15,868
Same grades.

10
00:00:15,918 --> 00:00:19,108
At 25, they even start at the same company.

11
00:00:19,158 --> 00:00:19,786
Same job.

12
00:00:19,836 --> 00:00:20,690
Same salary.

13
00:00:21,090 --> 00:00:23,650
Twenty years later…

14
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:26,417
…one of them is a millionaire.

15
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:30,447
The other one is still renting, and still in debt.

16
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:31,978
Ryan isn't smarter.

17
00:00:32,028 --> 00:00:34,073
He didn't win the lottery.

18
00:00:34,123 --> 00:00:35,685
He didn't get lucky.

19
00:00:35,735 --> 00:00:40,923
He just learned something Jake never did: how money really works.

20
00:00:40,973 --> 00:00:42,535
What the banks know.

21
00:00:42,585 --> 00:00:44,066
What the rich know.

22
00:00:44,116 --> 00:00:48,659
And in the next 15 minutes, you're going to learn it too.

23
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,973
It starts with Jake's first paycheck.

24
00:00:55,023 --> 00:00:56,691
He puts it in the bank.

25
00:00:56,741 --> 00:00:57,065
Safe.

26
00:00:57,115 --> 00:00:59,529
Exactly where you're supposed to.

27
00:00:59,579 --> 00:01:01,920
At least, that's what he thinks.

28
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:08,317
Here's the first thing nobody tells you: The moment you deposit money, it stops

29
00:01:08,367 --> 00:01:09,235
being yours…

30
00:01:09,285 --> 00:01:10,843
in the way you think.

31
00:01:10,893 --> 00:01:14,593
Legally, you've just lent your money to the bank.

32
00:01:14,643 --> 00:01:18,803
Your balance isn't a pile of cash with your name on it.

33
00:01:18,853 --> 00:01:19,952
It's a promise.

34
00:01:20,002 --> 00:01:21,330
The bank owes you.

35
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:26,266
And the bank doesn't keep your money lying around.

36
00:01:26,316 --> 00:01:27,275
It uses it.

37
00:01:27,325 --> 00:01:28,100
Lends it.

38
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:29,109
Invests it.

39
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:34,658
While Jake sleeps, his paycheck is already working, for the bank.

40
00:01:34,708 --> 00:01:40,577
It might be financing a stranger's car at seven or eight percent interest.

41
00:01:40,627 --> 00:01:43,136
Jake gets almost nothing for it.

42
00:01:43,186 --> 00:01:45,535
The bank keeps the difference.

43
00:01:45,585 --> 00:01:46,255
And Jake?

44
00:01:46,305 --> 00:01:48,735
He doesn't even know it's gone.

45
00:01:48,785 --> 00:01:50,894
His app still shows $5,000.

46
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:54,013
Because the app doesn't show the money.

47
00:01:54,063 --> 00:01:55,693
It shows the promise.

48
00:01:55,743 --> 00:02:02,251
For decades, American banks at least had to keep a small part of their deposits in

49
00:02:02,301 --> 00:02:02,891
reserve.

50
00:02:02,941 --> 00:02:07,050
In March 2020, that requirement was lowered to zero.

51
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:08,090
Zero percent.

52
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:13,483
So how much money is actually sitting in the vault, waiting for Jake?

53
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:15,237
Far less than he thinks.

54
00:02:15,287 --> 00:02:18,088
But that's not even the strangest part.

55
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:21,889
Because the bank doesn't just lend out Jake's money.

56
00:02:21,939 --> 00:02:24,740
It can create money that never existed.

57
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:28,537
A few years later, Ryan buys his first house.

58
00:02:28,587 --> 00:02:31,524
He takes out a 300,000-dollar mortgage.

59
00:02:31,574 --> 00:02:37,500
Most people imagine the bank opens its vault, takes 300,000 dollars that other

60
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:40,181
customers saved, and hands it over.

61
00:02:40,231 --> 00:02:42,020
That's not what happens.

62
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:46,574
The bank types 300,000 dollars into existence.

63
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:49,865
One second, the money doesn't exist.

64
00:02:49,915 --> 00:02:52,150
The next second, it does.

65
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,984
This isn't a conspiracy theory.

66
00:02:55,034 --> 00:03:02,571
The Bank of England, one of the oldest central banks in the world, said it in plain

67
00:03:02,621 --> 00:03:09,427
language in 2014: The majority of money in the modern economy is created by

68
00:03:09,477 --> 00:03:10,981
commercial banks…

69
00:03:11,031 --> 00:03:12,169
making loans.

70
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:14,377
And it works in reverse.

71
00:03:14,427 --> 00:03:20,569
Every month, when Ryan pays back his mortgage, that money doesn't go back into a

72
00:03:20,619 --> 00:03:21,033
vault.

73
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:22,039
It's deleted.

74
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:24,306
Think about what this means.

75
00:03:24,356 --> 00:03:28,550
Almost every dollar in the system was born as somebody's debt.

76
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:33,958
If everyone paid back every debt tomorrow, most of the money in the world would

77
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:35,121
simply disappear.

78
00:03:35,171 --> 00:03:36,079
Money is debt.

79
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:43,181
By the end of 2025, American households owed about 18.8 trillion dollars.

80
00:03:43,231 --> 00:03:44,106
Mortgages.

81
00:03:44,156 --> 00:03:45,031
Car loans.

82
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:46,325
Student loans.

83
00:03:46,375 --> 00:03:47,528
Credit cards.

84
00:03:47,578 --> 00:03:52,429
And now look at Ryan's mortgage from the bank's side.

85
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:58,462
300,000 dollars, over 30 years, at seven percent interest.

86
00:03:58,512 --> 00:04:06,397
By the time Ryan has paid it off, he'll have paid the bank about 718,000 dollars.

87
00:04:06,447 --> 00:04:10,610
More than 400,000 of that is just interest.

88
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:12,390
Let that sink in.

89
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:16,765
The bank created the 300,000 dollars out of nothing…

90
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:21,982
and will collect more than 400,000 dollars in interest for it.

91
00:04:22,032 --> 00:04:23,749
And if money is debt…

92
00:04:23,799 --> 00:04:28,124
then someone is making a fortune from all that debt.

93
00:04:28,174 --> 00:04:30,060
Want to guess who else?

94
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:34,192
Let's look at Jake's money from the bank's side.

95
00:04:34,242 --> 00:04:38,289
Jake keeps his savings in a regular savings account.

96
00:04:38,339 --> 00:04:42,544
For that, many big banks pay him a tiny interest rate.

97
00:04:42,594 --> 00:04:44,829
Often well under one percent.

98
00:04:44,879 --> 00:04:49,556
At the same time, Jake carries a balance on his credit card.

99
00:04:49,606 --> 00:04:55,860
In 2025, the average credit card interest rate in America was around 21 percent.

100
00:04:56,260 --> 00:05:00,706
So the bank borrows Jake's money for almost nothing…

101
00:05:00,756 --> 00:05:06,154
and lends money back to people like Jake for twenty times more.

102
00:05:06,204 --> 00:05:10,910
That gap is one of the oldest business models on Earth.

103
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:16,342
And the card companies have a favorite trick: the minimum payment.

104
00:05:16,392 --> 00:05:22,504
Say Jake owes 5,000 dollars at 21 percent, and only pays the minimum each month.

105
00:05:22,554 --> 00:05:26,046
It can take him around 19 years to pay it off.

106
00:05:26,096 --> 00:05:29,743
And he'll pay roughly 7,700 dollars in interest.

107
00:05:29,793 --> 00:05:31,900
More than the original debt.

108
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:38,940
And if you think that's bad, the price of money gets even worse the poorer you are.

109
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:45,470
A typical payday loan charges 15 dollars for every 100 dollars borrowed, for just

110
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,276
two weeks.

111
00:05:46,326 --> 00:05:52,966
According to America's consumer protection agency, that works out to an annual rate

112
00:05:53,016 --> 00:05:54,740
of almost 400 percent.

113
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:58,689
But there's another one most people never notice.

114
00:05:59,090 --> 00:06:03,355
Every time you pay with a card, the store pays a fee.

115
00:06:03,405 --> 00:06:05,391
Often around two percent.

116
00:06:05,441 --> 00:06:11,335
In 2024, American businesses paid about 187 billion dollars in card fees.

117
00:06:11,385 --> 00:06:15,000
And where do you think stores get that money?

118
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:16,054
From you.

119
00:06:16,104 --> 00:06:18,166
It's built into the prices.

120
00:06:18,216 --> 00:06:22,313
Even when you pay cash, you pay for everyone's cards.

121
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:25,051
The bank makes money when you save.

122
00:06:25,101 --> 00:06:27,477
It makes money when you borrow.

123
00:06:27,527 --> 00:06:29,746
It makes money when you shop.

124
00:06:29,796 --> 00:06:33,658
But the biggest money machine of all isn't a bank.

125
00:06:33,708 --> 00:06:36,710
It's the place where new money is born.

126
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:40,952
This is the Federal Reserve, America's central bank.

127
00:06:41,002 --> 00:06:46,790
When there's a crisis, the Fed can create money on a scale no normal bank ever

128
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:47,239
could.

129
00:06:47,289 --> 00:06:49,110
No printing press needed.

130
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:54,350
It buys bonds from banks and pays with money it creates on a computer.

131
00:06:54,750 --> 00:07:02,496
Before the 2008 financial crisis, everything the Fed owned was worth under 900

132
00:07:02,546 --> 00:07:04,096
billion dollars.

133
00:07:04,146 --> 00:07:07,494
By 2022, it was almost 9 trillion.

134
00:07:07,544 --> 00:07:08,994
Ten times more.

135
00:07:09,044 --> 00:07:17,090
When the pandemic hit, it jumped in just one year from about 4.2 trillion to more

136
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:18,890
than 7.5 trillion.

137
00:07:19,290 --> 00:07:26,247
If you spent a million dollars every single day, it would take you more than 20,000

138
00:07:26,297 --> 00:07:28,442
years to spend 8 trillion.

139
00:07:28,492 --> 00:07:31,059
So where did all that money go?

140
00:07:31,109 --> 00:07:35,450
Here's the part that changes how you see everything.

141
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:39,355
New money doesn't arrive everywhere at once.

142
00:07:39,405 --> 00:07:41,052
It enters at the top.

143
00:07:41,102 --> 00:07:42,183
Through banks.

144
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:44,285
Through financial markets.

145
00:07:44,335 --> 00:07:50,426
The people closest to it get it first, and they spend it before prices rise.

146
00:07:50,476 --> 00:07:52,608
Everyone else gets it last.

147
00:07:52,658 --> 00:07:59,154
Economists call this the Cantillon effect, named after an 18th-century banker who

148
00:07:59,204 --> 00:08:01,174
noticed it 300 years ago.

149
00:08:01,224 --> 00:08:04,730
And in 2020, you could watch it happen live.

150
00:08:05,130 --> 00:08:10,687
American home prices rose more than 30 percent in just two years.

151
00:08:10,737 --> 00:08:16,035
The stock market doubled from its 2020 low in under 18 months.

152
00:08:16,085 --> 00:08:19,054
People who owned houses and stocks…

153
00:08:19,104 --> 00:08:19,917
like Ryan…

154
00:08:19,967 --> 00:08:22,850
got richer without doing anything.

155
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:29,273
Ryan's 300,000-dollar house was suddenly worth around 390,000.

156
00:08:29,323 --> 00:08:35,151
He gained about 90,000 dollars in two years, while sleeping.

157
00:08:35,201 --> 00:08:39,070
More than his salary for an entire year.

158
00:08:39,470 --> 00:08:41,501
People who owned nothing…

159
00:08:41,551 --> 00:08:42,334
like Jake…

160
00:08:42,384 --> 00:08:46,164
just watched everything become more expensive.

161
00:08:46,214 --> 00:08:47,163
Same salary.

162
00:08:47,213 --> 00:08:48,162
Same street.

163
00:08:48,212 --> 00:08:50,493
Completely different result.

164
00:08:50,543 --> 00:08:56,655
And there's one more force quietly working against Jake, every single day.

165
00:08:56,705 --> 00:08:58,570
One he never agreed to.

166
00:08:58,970 --> 00:09:03,091
In 1971, 100 dollars could fill a cart like this.

167
00:09:03,141 --> 00:09:08,030
Today, to buy the same things, you need about 800 dollars.

168
00:09:08,430 --> 00:09:10,383
Nobody stole your money.

169
00:09:10,433 --> 00:09:12,469
It's still the same bill.

170
00:09:12,519 --> 00:09:13,971
It just buys less.

171
00:09:14,021 --> 00:09:14,890
Every year.

172
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:16,308
That's inflation.

173
00:09:16,358 --> 00:09:21,400
And here's what most people never hear: It's not an accident.

174
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:28,791
The Federal Reserve officially aims for prices to rise by about two percent every

175
00:09:28,841 --> 00:09:29,226
year.

176
00:09:29,276 --> 00:09:31,399
Two percent sounds small.

177
00:09:31,449 --> 00:09:37,920
But over 35 years, two percent a year cuts what your money can buy in half.

178
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:44,655
So Jake, saving responsibly in his bank account at under one percent…

179
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:48,171
is actually getting poorer every year.

180
00:09:48,221 --> 00:09:48,819
Slowly.

181
00:09:48,869 --> 00:09:49,560
Quietly.

182
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:54,919
Let's say Jake has saved 20,000 dollars, earning half a percent.

183
00:09:54,969 --> 00:10:01,259
If prices rise by three percent that year, his savings lose around 500 dollars in

184
00:10:01,309 --> 00:10:02,120
real value.

185
00:10:02,170 --> 00:10:03,059
In one year.

186
00:10:03,109 --> 00:10:05,799
Without him spending a single cent.

187
00:10:05,849 --> 00:10:12,295
Do that for ten years, and Jake has worked months of his life for money that simply

188
00:10:12,345 --> 00:10:13,234
melted away.

189
00:10:13,284 --> 00:10:13,939
And Ryan?

190
00:10:13,989 --> 00:10:17,852
His house and his stocks tend to rise with prices.

191
00:10:17,902 --> 00:10:19,496
Inflation melts cash.

192
00:10:19,546 --> 00:10:22,392
And it rewards people who own things.

193
00:10:22,442 --> 00:10:28,575
Which brings us to the question you've been waiting for: How do the really rich

194
00:10:28,625 --> 00:10:29,750
play this game?

195
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:34,396
Jake earns money, pays tax on it, and saves what's left.

196
00:10:34,446 --> 00:10:36,928
Ryan earns money and buys assets.

197
00:10:36,978 --> 00:10:40,918
But the richest people on Earth go one step further.

198
00:10:40,968 --> 00:10:43,220
They barely use income at all.

199
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:47,623
Their wealth sits in shares of their companies.

200
00:10:47,673 --> 00:10:51,158
And shares that aren't sold aren't taxed.

201
00:10:51,208 --> 00:10:56,160
So when they need money for a house, a jet, a new company…

202
00:10:56,210 --> 00:10:57,540
they don't sell.

203
00:10:57,940 --> 00:10:58,759
They borrow.

204
00:10:58,809 --> 00:11:01,148
Using their shares as collateral.

205
00:11:01,198 --> 00:11:02,597
A loan isn't income.

206
00:11:02,647 --> 00:11:04,769
So it isn't taxed like income.

207
00:11:04,819 --> 00:11:06,507
The shares keep growing.

208
00:11:06,557 --> 00:11:08,970
The loans get paid with new loans.

209
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:13,543
Imagine someone with one billion dollars in shares.

210
00:11:13,593 --> 00:11:20,003
If those shares grow eight percent in a year, that's 80 million dollars of new

211
00:11:20,053 --> 00:11:20,583
wealth.

212
00:11:20,633 --> 00:11:23,399
They borrow 10 million to live on.

213
00:11:23,449 --> 00:11:27,457
At five percent, the interest is 500,000 dollars.

214
00:11:27,507 --> 00:11:28,285
No salary.

215
00:11:28,335 --> 00:11:28,948
No sale.

216
00:11:28,998 --> 00:11:31,929
And no income tax on the 10 million.

217
00:11:31,979 --> 00:11:35,739
The wealth keeps growing faster than the debt.

218
00:11:35,789 --> 00:11:37,230
And when they die?

219
00:11:37,630 --> 00:11:42,905
Under American tax law, their heirs inherit those shares at today's value.

220
00:11:42,955 --> 00:11:48,446
All the gains that built up over a lifetime are never taxed as capital gains.

221
00:11:48,496 --> 00:11:51,468
In finance, this has a dark nickname: Buy.

222
00:11:51,518 --> 00:11:51,972
Borrow.

223
00:11:52,022 --> 00:11:52,260
Die.

224
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:58,331
In 2021, the newsroom ProPublica got hold of secret IRS data.

225
00:11:58,381 --> 00:12:06,022
They compared what the 25 richest Americans paid in taxes to how much their wealth

226
00:12:06,072 --> 00:12:06,491
grew.

227
00:12:06,541 --> 00:12:13,994
Between 2014 and 2018, that was what ProPublica called a "true tax rate" of just

228
00:12:14,044 --> 00:12:15,120
3.4 percent.

229
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:16,367
It's legal.

230
00:12:16,417 --> 00:12:18,569
It's just a different game.

231
00:12:18,619 --> 00:12:23,790
And it all runs on one thing Jake has been taught to fear: debt.

232
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:27,538
Here's the cruelest part of the whole system.

233
00:12:27,588 --> 00:12:28,897
Money has a price.

234
00:12:28,947 --> 00:12:30,483
It's called interest.

235
00:12:30,533 --> 00:12:33,731
And the price is not the same for everyone.

236
00:12:33,781 --> 00:12:37,280
Jake pays around 21 percent on his credit card.

237
00:12:37,330 --> 00:12:43,096
The billionaire, borrowing against millions in shares, can often borrow for a

238
00:12:43,146 --> 00:12:44,380
fraction of that.

239
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:48,580
The less money you have, the more money costs you.

240
00:12:48,630 --> 00:12:52,276
The more money you have, the cheaper money gets.

241
00:12:52,326 --> 00:12:55,203
And look at what each one borrows for.

242
00:12:55,253 --> 00:12:58,514
Jake borrows to buy things that lose value.

243
00:12:58,564 --> 00:12:58,899
A TV.

244
00:12:58,949 --> 00:12:59,361
A car.

245
00:12:59,411 --> 00:13:00,208
A vacation.

246
00:13:00,258 --> 00:13:03,751
The rich borrow to buy things that gain value.

247
00:13:03,801 --> 00:13:04,521
Companies.

248
00:13:04,571 --> 00:13:05,291
Buildings.

249
00:13:05,341 --> 00:13:05,830
Shares.

250
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:08,294
For most people, debt is a trap.

251
00:13:08,344 --> 00:13:12,299
For the people who understand money, debt is a tool.

252
00:13:12,349 --> 00:13:13,146
Same thing.

253
00:13:13,196 --> 00:13:14,378
Opposite result.

254
00:13:14,428 --> 00:13:20,000
But what happens when the people playing with all this debt get it wrong?

255
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:21,801
March 9th, 2023.

256
00:13:21,851 --> 00:13:26,970
Silicon Valley Bank, one of the biggest banks in America.

257
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:28,421
A rumor spreads.

258
00:13:28,471 --> 00:13:30,235
Group chats explode.

259
00:13:30,285 --> 00:13:32,140
"Get your money out."

260
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:38,157
In a single day, customers tried to withdraw 42 billion dollars.

261
00:13:38,207 --> 00:13:40,903
But remember Secret Number One.

262
00:13:40,953 --> 00:13:43,560
The money was never all there.

263
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:47,824
The next morning, regulators shut the bank down.

264
00:13:47,874 --> 00:13:52,717
In America, deposits are only insured up to 250,000 dollars.

265
00:13:52,767 --> 00:13:56,958
Most of the money at this bank was above that limit.

266
00:13:57,008 --> 00:14:02,749
And yet, two days later, the government guaranteed every single dollar.

267
00:14:02,799 --> 00:14:04,461
Even above the limit.

268
00:14:04,511 --> 00:14:04,788
Why?

269
00:14:04,838 --> 00:14:10,660
Because if trust breaks at one bank, people start running from the next.

270
00:14:11,060 --> 00:14:12,921
We've seen this before.

271
00:14:12,971 --> 00:14:18,324
In 2008, when the big banks gambled and lost, Congress approved a

272
00:14:18,374 --> 00:14:21,150
700-billion-dollar rescue program.

273
00:14:21,550 --> 00:14:27,502
And a few months later, the insurance giant AIG, which had just been rescued with

274
00:14:27,552 --> 00:14:33,430
taxpayer money, paid out around 165 million dollars in bonuses to its employees.

275
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:39,506
According to New York's attorney general, 73 of them received at least one million

276
00:14:39,556 --> 00:14:40,470
dollars each.

277
00:14:40,870 --> 00:14:45,148
When the system wins, the profits are private.

278
00:14:45,198 --> 00:14:50,042
When it loses, the losses become everyone's problem.

279
00:14:50,092 --> 00:14:51,548
Heads, they win.

280
00:14:51,598 --> 00:14:52,113
Tails…

281
00:14:52,163 --> 00:14:52,960
you lose.

282
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:56,194
So let's go back to Jake and Ryan.

283
00:14:56,244 --> 00:14:57,212
Same street.

284
00:14:57,262 --> 00:14:58,230
Same school.

285
00:14:58,280 --> 00:14:59,248
Same salary.

286
00:14:59,298 --> 00:15:01,963
Jake did everything he was told.

287
00:15:02,013 --> 00:15:03,151
He saved cash.

288
00:15:03,201 --> 00:15:05,102
He borrowed for things.

289
00:15:05,152 --> 00:15:08,326
He trusted the bank to keep his money.

290
00:15:08,376 --> 00:15:10,278
Ryan learned the rules.

291
00:15:10,328 --> 00:15:11,720
Cash loses value.

292
00:15:11,770 --> 00:15:13,332
So he owned things.

293
00:15:13,382 --> 00:15:16,047
New money flows to assets first.

294
00:15:16,097 --> 00:15:17,659
So he owned assets.

295
00:15:17,709 --> 00:15:18,931
Debt is a tool.

296
00:15:18,981 --> 00:15:22,580
So he only used it to buy things that grow.

297
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:27,269
And here's the part that hurts the most.

298
00:15:27,319 --> 00:15:33,704
If Jake had simply put 300 dollars a month into a broad stock market fund from age

299
00:15:33,754 --> 00:15:39,825
25, and it had grown at the market's long-term average of around ten percent a

300
00:15:39,875 --> 00:15:46,104
year, which is never guaranteed, by 45 he would have had around 228,000 dollars.

301
00:15:46,154 --> 00:15:49,400
From just 72,000 dollars of his own money.

302
00:15:49,450 --> 00:15:50,106
The rest?

303
00:15:50,156 --> 00:15:53,088
The same force the rich use every day.

304
00:15:53,138 --> 00:15:54,972
Owning things that grow.

305
00:15:55,022 --> 00:15:57,876
Neither of them did anything illegal.

306
00:15:57,926 --> 00:16:00,073
Neither of them was smarter.

307
00:16:00,123 --> 00:16:04,389
The only difference was knowing how money really works.

308
00:16:04,790 --> 00:16:06,938
Look at that dollar again.

309
00:16:06,988 --> 00:16:08,628
Four cents of paper.

310
00:16:08,678 --> 00:16:11,756
Most money like it was never printed.

311
00:16:11,806 --> 00:16:16,744
Only typed into existence, the moment someone took on debt.

312
00:16:16,794 --> 00:16:20,126
And it's worth a little less every year.

313
00:16:20,176 --> 00:16:25,030
It works for one reason only: we all keep believing in it.

314
00:16:25,430 --> 00:16:28,070
Now you know the rules.

315
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,760
Most people never will.

316
00:16:31,160 --> 00:16:34,585
If this changed how you see money, subscribe.

317
00:16:34,635 --> 00:16:38,370
There's a lot more the banks will never tell you.
